To rotate a point $(x, y)$ by $45^\circ$ clockwise about the origin, we use the rotation matrix: \[
R = \begin{pmatrix}
\cos(-45^\circ) & -\sin(-45^\circ) \\
\sin(-45^\circ) & \cos(-45^\circ)
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
\cos(45^\circ) & \sin(45^\circ) \\
-\sin(45^\circ) & \cos(45^\circ)
\end{pmatrix}.
\] Since $\cos(45^\circ) = \sin(45^\circ) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$, the matrix becomes: \[
R = \begin{pmatrix}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\
-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\end{pmatrix}.
\] The matrix that corresponds to rotating about the origin by an angle of $45^\circ$ clockwise is \boxed{\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\
-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\end{pmatrix}}. </think> <answer> \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \end{pmatrix}} </answer>

The rotation matrix for an angle $\theta$ in the clockwise direction is given by
\[
R(\theta) = \begin{pmatrix}
\cos(-\theta) & -\sin(-\theta) \\
\sin(-\theta) & \cos(-\theta)
\end{pmatrix}
\]
Since we are rotating by $45^\circ$ clockwise, $\theta = -45^\circ$. Thus,
\[
R(-45^\circ) = \begin{pmatrix}
\cos(45^\circ) & \sin(45^\circ) \\
-\sin(45^\circ) & \cos(45^\circ)
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\
-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
\end{pmatrix}
\]
